# Selecto Domain Join Types Workbook

## Setup 1) Install Dependencies

Run this cell first. It installs the local `selecto_livebooks` project and its
current Selecto dependencies for this Livebook session.

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install!()
```

## Overview

This workbook explores advanced domain-level join intent markers.

1. `:star_dimension` for OLAP-like aggregate semantics
2. `:snowflake_dimension` for normalized dimension chains
3. Hierarchical markers (`:adjacency_list`, `:materialized_path`, `:closure_table`)
4. Metadata and SQL differences for the same logical query

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  base[Base domain join]
  marker[Join type marker]
  planner[Selecto planner]
  sql[Generated SQL + metadata]

  base --> marker --> planner --> sql
```

```mermaid
graph TD
  fact[orders fact]
  customer[customer dimension]
  snow[snowflake normalization]
  hier[hierarchical join family]

  fact --> customer
  customer --> snow
  customer --> hier
```

Before running, initialize the sample database:

```bash
mix setup
```

## Setup 2) Connect to Shared Repo and Load Base Domains

This cell starts the shared sample-app repo and loads the maintained order and
employee domains for join-type variants.

```elixir
config = SelectoLivebooks.NotebookSupport.config([:order_domain, :employee_domain])
counts = SelectoLivebooks.NotebookSupport.table_counts(["orders", "employees"])
order_count = counts["orders"]
employee_count = counts["employees"]

IO.puts("Connected. Orders: #{order_count}, Employees: #{employee_count}")

config
```

## Setup 3) Define Join-Type Helper

This helper prints SQL and optionally executes the query.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

defmodule JoinTypeLab.Helpers do
  @moduledoc false

  def explain(label, query), do: Selecto.Livebook.explain(label, query)

  def run(label, query, preview_count \\ 10) do
    Selecto.Livebook.run(label, query, preview_count: preview_count)
  end
end
```

## 1) `:star_dimension` Marker

Switch only the customer join type marker and run an aggregate query.

```elixir
olap_domain =
  put_in(config.order_domain, [:joins, :customer, :type], :star_dimension)

olap_query =
  Selecto.configure(olap_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(["customer.name", {:count, "*"}, {:sum, "total"}])
  |> Selecto.group_by(["customer.name"])
  |> Selecto.order_by({{:sum, "total"}, :desc})
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

JoinTypeLab.Helpers.run("Star dimension marker", olap_query)
```

## 2) `:snowflake_dimension` Marker

Apply the snowflake marker and normalization chain while keeping query API usage unchanged.

```elixir
snowflake_domain =
  put_in(config.order_domain, [:joins, :customer],
    config.order_domain.joins.customer
    |> Map.merge(%{
      type: :snowflake_dimension,
      display_field: :name,
      normalization_joins: [
        %{table: "customers", key: "id", foreign_key: "id", alias: "customer_customers"}
      ]
    })
  )

snowflake_selecto = Selecto.configure(snowflake_domain, config.repo)
snowflake_join = Selecto.joins(snowflake_selecto)[:customer]

snowflake_query =
  snowflake_selecto
  |> Selecto.select(["customer.name", {:count, "*"}, {:sum, "total"}])
  |> Selecto.group_by(["customer.name"])
  |> Selecto.order_by({{:sum, "total"}, :desc})
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

IO.puts("Join metadata marker: #{inspect(snowflake_join.join_type)}")
JoinTypeLab.Helpers.run("Snowflake dimension marker", snowflake_query)
```

## 3) Side-by-Side Marker Comparison (`:left`, `:star_dimension`, `:snowflake_dimension`)

Keep the same select/filter/order query and compare SQL + metadata by marker only.

```elixir
base_customer_join = config.order_domain.joins.customer

regular_domain =
  put_in(config.order_domain, [:joins, :customer],
    base_customer_join
    |> Map.put(:type, :left)
  )

star_domain =
  put_in(config.order_domain, [:joins, :customer],
    base_customer_join
    |> Map.merge(%{type: :star_dimension, display_field: :name})
  )

snowflake_domain_compare =
  put_in(config.order_domain, [:joins, :customer],
    base_customer_join
    |> Map.merge(%{
      type: :snowflake_dimension,
      display_field: :name,
      normalization_joins: [
        %{table: "customers", key: "id", foreign_key: "id", alias: "customer_customers"}
      ]
    })
  )

build_query = fn selecto ->
  selecto
  |> Selecto.select(["order_number", "customer.name", "total"])
  |> Selecto.order_by({"total", :desc})
  |> Selecto.limit(5)
end

Enum.each(
  [
    {"Regular (:left)", regular_domain},
    {"Star (:star_dimension)", star_domain},
    {"Snowflake (:snowflake_dimension)", snowflake_domain_compare}
  ],
  fn {label, domain} ->
    selecto = Selecto.configure(domain, config.repo)
    query = build_query.(selecto)
    {_sql, _params} = JoinTypeLab.Helpers.explain(label, query)
    join_meta = Selecto.joins(selecto)[:customer]
    IO.puts("join_type metadata: #{inspect(Map.get(join_meta, :join_type, :basic))}")
  end
)

:ok
```

## 4) Hierarchical Marker Metadata Preview

Preview metadata and generated field surfaces for three hierarchy styles without requiring all physical schema artifacts.

```elixir
hierarchy_base =
  config.employee_domain
  |> put_in([:source, :associations, :manager_tree], %{
    field: :manager_tree,
    queryable: :employees,
    owner_key: :manager_id,
    related_key: :id
  })

hierarchy_variants = [
  {
    :adjacency_list,
    %{
      name: "Manager Tree",
      type: :hierarchical,
      hierarchy_type: :adjacency_list,
      depth_limit: 5,
      id_field: :id,
      parent_field: :manager_id,
      name_field: :first_name,
      fields: %{first_name: %{type: :string}, last_name: %{type: :string}}
    }
  },
  {
    :materialized_path,
    %{
      name: "Manager Tree",
      type: :hierarchical,
      hierarchy_type: :materialized_path,
      path_field: :manager_path,
      path_separator: "/",
      fields: %{first_name: %{type: :string}, last_name: %{type: :string}}
    }
  },
  {
    :closure_table,
    %{
      name: "Manager Tree",
      type: :hierarchical,
      hierarchy_type: :closure_table,
      closure_table: "employee_closure",
      ancestor_field: :ancestor_id,
      descendant_field: :descendant_id,
      depth_field: :depth,
      fields: %{first_name: %{type: :string}, last_name: %{type: :string}}
    }
  }
]

Enum.each(hierarchy_variants, fn {label, join_config} ->
  domain = put_in(hierarchy_base, [:joins, :manager_tree], join_config)

  try do
    selecto = Selecto.configure(domain, config.repo)
    join_meta = Selecto.joins(selecto)[:manager_tree]

    hierarchy_fields =
      selecto
      |> Selecto.columns()
      |> Map.keys()
      |> Enum.filter(&String.contains?(&1, "manager_tree"))
      |> Enum.sort()
      |> Enum.take(12)

    IO.puts("\n=== #{label} ===")
    IO.puts("join_type: #{inspect(join_meta.join_type)}")
    IO.puts("hierarchy_depth: #{inspect(Map.get(join_meta, :hierarchy_depth))}")
    IO.puts("path_field: #{inspect(Map.get(join_meta, :path_field))}")
    IO.puts("closure_table: #{inspect(Map.get(join_meta, :closure_table))}")
    IO.puts("field preview: #{inspect(hierarchy_fields)}")
  rescue
    e ->
      IO.puts("\n=== #{label} ===")
      IO.puts("Hierarchy preview failed: #{Exception.message(e)}")
  end
end)

:ok
```

## 5) Adjacency Query Runtime (`:adjacency_list`)

Run a small adjacency-list hierarchy query against the sample dataset.

```elixir
adjacency_domain =
  hierarchy_base
  |> put_in([:joins, :manager_tree], %{
    name: "Manager Tree",
    type: :hierarchical,
    hierarchy_type: :adjacency_list,
    depth_limit: 5,
    id_field: :id,
    parent_field: :manager_id,
    name_field: :first_name,
    fields: %{first_name: %{type: :string}, last_name: %{type: :string}}
  })

adjacency_query =
  Selecto.configure(adjacency_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select([
    "id",
    "first_name",
    "last_name",
    "manager_tree.first_name",
    "manager_tree_level",
    "manager_tree_path"
  ])
  |> Selecto.limit(15)

JoinTypeLab.Helpers.run("Adjacency hierarchy runtime", adjacency_query)
```

## 7) Snowflake Normalization Chains

Build a reporting query that walks from orders through customer and geography
style dimensions without flattening the domain definition into one table.

```elixir
snowflake_chain_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([
    order_number,
    customer.name,
    customer.tier,
    customer.country,
    shipping_country,
    total
  ]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(customer.active == true))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([asc(customer.country), desc(total)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(20)

JoinTypeLab.Helpers.run("Snowflake-style customer geography chain", snowflake_chain_query)
```

## 8) Compare SQL Plans Between Join Markers

Run `EXPLAIN` for representative join-marker queries and compare their top-level
plan text.

```elixir
plan_rows =
  [
    {"snowflake", snowflake_chain_query},
    {"adjacency", adjacency_query}
  ]
  |> Enum.map(fn {label, query} ->
    case Selecto.explain(query, format: :text) do
      {:ok, %{plan_lines: plan_lines}} ->
        %{label: label, plan: Enum.join(plan_lines, "\n")}

      {:error, error} ->
        %{label: label, plan: inspect(error)}
    end
  end)

Kino.DataTable.new(plan_rows)
```

## 9) Hierarchy Reporting with Window Functions

Combine hierarchy-derived fields with a window rank to create an org-reporting
slice.

```elixir
hierarchy_rank_query =
  adjacency_query
  |> Selecto.window_function(:row_number, [],
    over: [order_by: [{"last_name", :asc}]],
    as: "level_name_rank"
  )
  |> Selecto.order_by({"manager_tree_level", :asc})
  |> Selecto.order_by({"last_name", :asc})
  |> Selecto.limit(30)

JoinTypeLab.Helpers.run("Hierarchy marker with level rank", hierarchy_rank_query)
```

## 10) Join Marker Configuration Matrix

Put marker configuration maps side by side when choosing between ordinary joins,
snowflake chains, and adjacency hierarchies.

```elixir
join_marker_matrix =
  [
    %{marker: "ordinary", source: "customers", relationship: "orders.customer_id -> customers.id", generated_fields: "joined table fields"},
    %{marker: "snowflake", source: "manager chain", relationship: "employee.manager_id -> manager.id", generated_fields: "each configured hop"},
    %{marker: "adjacency", source: "employees", relationship: "id -> manager_id recursively", generated_fields: "level, path, root"}
  ]

Kino.DataTable.new(join_marker_matrix)
```

## 11) Hierarchy Rollups

Aggregate an adjacency-style employee tree to see how many people appear at each
reporting depth.

```elixir
{:ok, hierarchy_rollup} =
  Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, """
  WITH RECURSIVE manager_tree AS (
    SELECT id, manager_id, department, 0 AS level
    FROM employees
    WHERE manager_id IS NULL

    UNION ALL

    SELECT employee.id, employee.manager_id, employee.department, manager_tree.level + 1
    FROM employees employee
    JOIN manager_tree ON employee.manager_id = manager_tree.id
  )
  SELECT department, level, count(*) AS employee_count
  FROM manager_tree
  GROUP BY department, level
  ORDER BY department, level
  """, [])

hierarchy_rollup.rows
```

## 12) Missing Marker Field Troubleshooting

Use this checklist when a marker-generated field does not appear where a query
expects it.

```elixir
expected_marker_fields = ["manager_tree_level", "manager_tree_path", "manager_tree_root_id"]
selected_fields = ["id", "first_name", "last_name", "manager_tree_level"]

expected_marker_fields
|> Enum.map(fn field ->
  %{
    field: field,
    selected?: field in selected_fields,
    next_check:
      if(field in selected_fields,
        do: "field selected; inspect SQL alias",
        else: "add field to select list or marker projection"
      )
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## 13) Marker Matrix from Domain Metadata

Generate the marker overview from the domain maps instead of hand-maintaining
the comparison table.

```elixir
marker_metadata_rows =
  config.employee_domain.joins
  |> Enum.map(fn {join_id, join_config} ->
    %{
      join_id: join_id,
      type: Map.get(join_config, :type),
      source: Map.get(join_config, :source),
      owner_key: inspect(Map.get(join_config, :owner_key) || get_in(join_config, [:on, Access.at(0), :left])),
      related_key: inspect(Map.get(join_config, :related_key) || get_in(join_config, [:on, Access.at(0), :right]))
    }
  end)

Kino.DataTable.new(marker_metadata_rows)
```

## 14) Hierarchy Depth Rollups Over Time

Bucket employees by hierarchy depth and hire year to inspect reporting-shape
changes over time.

```elixir
{:ok, depth_over_time} =
  Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, """
  WITH RECURSIVE manager_tree AS (
    SELECT id, manager_id, hire_date, 0 AS level
    FROM employees
    WHERE manager_id IS NULL

    UNION ALL

    SELECT employee.id, employee.manager_id, employee.hire_date, manager_tree.level + 1
    FROM employees employee
    JOIN manager_tree ON employee.manager_id = manager_tree.id
  )
  SELECT level, date_part('year', hire_date)::int AS hire_year, count(*) AS employee_count
  FROM manager_tree
  GROUP BY level, hire_year
  ORDER BY level, hire_year
  """, [])

depth_over_time.rows
```

## 15) Marker Alias Mistake Pair

Keep failing and fixed field paths side by side so readers can spot alias
mistakes before executing a query.

```elixir
alias_examples = [
  %{state: "failing", field: "manager_tree.level", reason: "uses dot path that is not a selected marker alias"},
  %{state: "fixed", field: "manager_tree_level", reason: "uses generated marker field name"}
]

Kino.DataTable.new(alias_examples)
```

